# Object Explorer & Filtering System

## Architecture Overview

```
ObjectExplorer              ← renders VirtualTreeItem tree from Speckle world tree
  ├─ useSpeckleTree         ← flattens tree, manages expansion/selection
  ├─ useSpeckleFiltering    ← thin wrapper over speckleStore (hide/show/isolate intent)
  └─ useSpeckleInteractions ← hover highlight, zoom-to

useAcousticLayerIsolation   ← auto-detects the acoustic layer, publishes its geometry-id
                               union, and triggers applyVisibility() (runs in SpeckleScene)
speckleStore.applyVisibility ← THE single place that applies hide/isolate to the viewer
```

## Row Geometry Units

A row's geometry ids come from `getGeometryLeafIdsFromNode(item.data)` (`useSpeckleTree.ts`).
A **display mesh is never its own unit**. The backend maps display-mesh ids onto the host's
whole face range (host = object with `displayValue`, e.g. `Objects.Data.DataObject`), and Speckle
colors a host together with all its descendant render views. `flattenModelTree` therefore sets
`data.geometryRoot = host` on display-mesh rows, both the single-child merge (the promoted
Mesh is shown under the host's name) and expanded children. `collectGeometryFromNode` walks from
`geometryRoot`. Without this, unassigning such a row cleared only the mesh id. The still-assigned
host kept it colored and in the simulation. Hosted objects (e.g. a wall's `elements`) are not in
`displayValue`, so they stay separate units.

## Unified Visibility Model (speckleStore)

FilteringExtension keeps ONE global `hiddenObjects` array and ONE global
`isolatedObjects` array (see `FilteringState`) — hide and isolate are mutually
exclusive, and a `stateKey`/command-family change resets the internal ids dict.
The app no longer fights this with two stateKeys + a rAF re-apply loop. Instead:

- **Intent** lives in speckleStore: `userHiddenIds`, `userIsolatedIds` (DV),
  `acousticExplorerHiddenIds` (AV "exclude").
- **`applyVisibility()`** derives a single target `{ hidden | isolated }` from
  `viewMode + acoustic layer + intent` and applies it **atomically**:
  `resetFilters()` → one `isolateObjects`/`hideObjects` on the single stateKey
  `'explorer'` → `applyFilterColors()` (restore user colors).
- Idempotent — compares against `_appliedHiddenIds`/`_appliedIsolatedIds`.
- `useSpeckleFiltering` is now just a thin wrapper that mutates intent and calls
  `applyVisibility()`; its `hiddenObjects`/`isolatedObjects` read the applied sets.
- **Auto-hide of unassigned objects** (`useAutoHideUnassignedObjects`, driven by
  `AcousticsSection`). It applies in Acoustics view to a generated, not-running, not-in-edit-mode
  pyroomacoustics/Choras card: the **powered-on** card first, else the **expanded** card.
  `syncAutoHiddenIds(cardId, ids)` writes the region's unassigned ids into
  `acousticExplorerHiddenIds`, so each one is re-showable with its eye.
  - Assignments come from the live material store when the card is the expanded one. Otherwise they
    come from its persisted `speckleMaterialAssignments`, expanded via `utils/speckle-assignment-ids.ts`.
  - Only ids it added are owned (`_autoHiddenIds`); manual hides are never restored by it.
  - A key change (other card / inactive) restores the owned ids. The same key applies only a diff,
    so a user re-show sticks until the card changes.
  - It runs inside `runWithoutUndo` (not undoable).
  - Edit mode comes from `Card`'s `onSettingsExpandedChange`.

## View Modes

| Mode      | Behavior |
|-----------|----------|
| `default` | If user isolated → `isolated = userIsolatedIds`; else `hidden = userHiddenIds ∪ acousticLayerIds`. Acoustic layer hidden from the viewer (shown in the tree). |
| `acoustic`| **Hide-based isolation** — `hidden = (all model geometry − acoustic layer) ∪ excluded`. The acoustic layer (minus excluded) is the ONLY thing visible; non-acoustic objects are fully hidden (not ghosted). |
| `dark`    | `applyFilterColors()` returns early. UI-only mode. |

Why hide-based in acoustic mode: `isolateObjects(ghost=true)` cannot express
"isolate the layer AND fully hide one excluded object" (hide/isolate are
mutually exclusive). Using `hideObjects(ghost=false)` lets "exclude" fully hide.

View mode is **not persisted** through Zustand `persist` — it's stored in a
separate `localStorage` key `compas-view-mode` and restored 300ms after viewer
init in `SpeckleScene.tsx`.

## FilteringExtension — Verified Semantics (@speckle/viewer@2.26.9)

Read from the minified bundle (`dist/index.js`). Command enum:
`HIDE=10, SHOW=11, ISOLATE=20, UNISOLATE=21, NONE=30`.

- `setVisibilityState` resets its internal `ids` dict when
  `stateKey` changes **or** `|command − prevCommand| > 1` (a family change).
  Same stateKey + same family = additive (HIDE/ISOLATE merge ids) or delete
  (SHOW/UNISOLATE remove only the given ids — NOT clear-all).
- `resetFilters()` clears `UserspaceColorState` (user colors) → you MUST re-run
  `applyFilterColors()` after it, or entity/material colors vanish.
- `setFilters()` applies HIDDEN/GHOST first, then **COLORED (user colors) LAST**.
  So a colored render view overrides its own hide — colors must be filtered
  against the hidden/isolated set.
- `hideObjects`/`isolateObjects`/`showObjects`/`unIsolateObjects`/`setUserObjectColors`
  AND `highlightObjects`/`resetHighlight`/`resetSelection`/`selectObjects` are all
  methods on the **Viewer**, NOT on `getRenderer()` (which is the SpeckleRenderer).
  `useSpeckleInteractions` must call `viewer.highlightObjects(ids)` /
  `viewer.resetHighlight()`, never `viewer.getRenderer().highlightObjects(...)`.

## Shared Render View (Brep + display Mesh)

A Brep and its display Mesh resolve to the SAME render view (`getRenderViewsForNodeId`
returns the same `guid`/`batchStart` for both — one `atomic:false`, one `atomic:true`).
When the cascade material assignment colors both, hiding the Brep leaves the display
Mesh in the color group, and COLORED (applied after HIDDEN) keeps the shared render
view visible. **Fix:** in `applyFilterColors`, exclude any material color id whose
render view is in the hide set (`filteringExt.VisibilityState.rvs` guids).

## Selection / Hover Override

The SelectionExtension and HighlightExtension apply materials AFTER the
FilteringExtension, so a selected/hovered object visually "reappears" after being
hidden. `applyVisibility()` clears both for now-hidden ids BEFORE applying the
filter (via `clearSelectionAndHighlight` — `SelectionExtension.clearSelection()` +
`viewer.resetHighlight()`).

## Multi-select Acoustic Layer

`acousticLayerStore` holds `selectedAcousticLayerIds`/`selectedAcousticLayerNames`
(lists; primary = first). The Object Explorer's selection mode multi-toggles layers,
previews via `setSelectionPreviewIds()`, and commits with `setAcousticLayers()`.
`useAcousticLayerIsolation` publishes the union of leaf ids via
`setAcousticLayerAllIds()` + `setAllModelGeometryIds()` and triggers
`applyVisibility()`. Simulation hand-off sends the union via
`resolveSimulationGeometryObjectIds()` → `geometry_object_ids`.

## Hide does NOT un-assign material

Hiding an object only changes visibility. Its acoustic material assignment stays
in `useAcousticMaterialStore.materialAssignments` (dropdown keeps its value, and it
is still sent to the backend simulation). Do not call `assignMaterialToObjects(id, '')`
from a hide handler.

## Key Files

| File | Role |
|------|------|
| `ObjectExplorer.tsx` | Tree UI; hide/isolate dispatch intent; multi-select acoustic layer |
| `useSpeckleFiltering.ts` | Thin wrapper over speckleStore's unified visibility model |
| `useAcousticLayerIsolation.ts` | Auto-detect layer, publish geometry-id union, trigger applyVisibility |
| `speckleStore.ts` | `applyVisibility`, `applyFilterColors`, intent + applied state |
| `acousticLayerStore.ts` | Persisted multi-select acoustic layer |
| `VirtualTreeItem.tsx` | Renders a single tree row with hide/isolate buttons |
